regress/artifacts/fbctrlcmdkill/pass2_20261018\grok_p2.txt:54:Its own commit, landed first. `e7fc97e6` put a promptless probe in the tree and left both pins behind, so gate 6 was already going to fail without `#567`. The deriver counts files it can see. Grandfather `sgi_mte_decline_probe.py` and `--write` while `fbctrl_cmd_probe.py` is not yet part of that census, commit that, then add `#567` and write 76 and 26. One commit makes 74→76 look like one cause, and reverting `#567` puts `#564`'s break back. The comment in `gate_hygiene.sh` tells the truth. The history should still be two commits.
regress/artifacts/fbctrlcmdkill/pass2_20261018\grok_p2.txt:64:(d) stands. `--write` will not move the pins while that probe is promptless and ungrandfathered. The 2026-09-19 precedent is the rule you already adopted. Converting its fixed waits is the filed follow-up.
regress/artifacts/fbctrlcmdkill/pass2_20261018\grok_p2.txt:66:(e) is the disposition I do not accept. Gate 16 is the MIPS fold witness. `#549` already put one framebuffer probe in front of the kernel check and filed `gatedevicesplit`. This block adds a second full pty probe and calls the growth recorded. Recording it is how the debt stays. The probe belongs on a device gate. I would still rather have it wired here than unwired: an unwired detector is the `#564` failure mode. The arm can land. The sentence that this placement is fine should not.
