regress/artifacts/fbctrlcmdkill/pass2_20261018\glm_p2.txt:3:VERDICT: SHIP WITH CHANGES -- the fix and instrument are sound (I re-derived all sixteen mutant scores from the inlined code and every one matches), but the latch-set × wide-alias cell is a real surviving hole the gate inherits, the census repair belongs in its own commit landed first, and the packet's own numstat disagrees with its inlined diff by one line.
regress/artifacts/fbctrlcmdkill/pass2_20261018\glm_p2.txt:11:FLAG (not checkable, with settling facts): (i) `memory_readmax64`'s body is not inlined -- but see Q1a, it is behaviorally pinned; (ii) the remainder of `dev_fbctrl.c` beyond the three inlined functions is absent -- one grep for assignments to `port[` in that file settles Q1b's "no other writer"; (iii) the header constants (`DEV_FBCTRL_COMMAND_GET_RESOLUTION == 2`, `DEV_FBCTRL_NPORTS > 5`) are not inlined -- behaviorally pinned by the bound rows and the green `live` row; (iv) whether the promptless census pin (43) is a literal or derived from the grandfather names list -- the "whole diff" contains no 43→44 hunk, so either the pin is derived (consistent with `--write: 76 and 26` naming only two numbers) or a hunk is missing from the packet.
regress/artifacts/fbctrlcmdkill/pass2_20261018\glm_p2.txt:32:**7. The census repair.** Its own commit, landed first. The deriver's refusal fixes the *order* (grandfather before `--write`), not the authorship; #564's debt is urgent independently (the next battery reds gate 6 with or without #567, so it must not wait on #567's adjudication -- if pass 2 holds the fix, the tree stays red for #564's reason); and one commit couples reverts: reverting #567 would revert the grandfather and pin moves and re-strand #564. The one real obstacle -- the joint line 74→76 carries both probes' +1 and cannot be half-committed -- is soluble: move #567's probe out of the tree temporarily, `--grandfather sgi_mte_decline_probe.py` + `--write` (the #564-only intermediate: 75/25 plus the names block), commit, restore the probe, re-derive to 76/26, commit #567. The tool reads the tree, not the diff. If the panel will not do that surgery, folding is acceptable only with the CHANGELOG recording that the gate_hygiene hunks are #564's repair riding in #567's commit and must survive a #567 revert.
regress/artifacts/fbctrlcmdkill/pass2_20261018\glm_p2.txt:34:**8. The five dispositions.** (a) ACCEPT (REASONED): E2 measured 92/95 with exactly the three NOP rows red, so the NOP cell is held alone; D4's masking is a compound-mutant artifact the packet itself diagnosed. (b) ACCEPT, with the note that the aliasing was foreseeable at writing time (the packet says so) and that the same mask exists under B_gateoff, which the acceptance did not name. (c) ACCEPT: measured at both nulls, and the equality pin plus CONTROL=OK converts the 15 absence-greens into "the probe ran everything" witnesses; this is clause (c) in its recorded measured form, and the replay confirms the gate reds on both nulls. (d) ACCEPT WITH TEETH: the grandfather follows the 2026-09-19 ruling and `ptyprobepins` closes the door behind it, but grandfathering is debt-tolerance, not repair -- the promptless fleet (now grown again) runs on fixed waits and will flake under load; `sgimtereadiness` is filed but nothing in this packet schedules it, and I would require an owner before the next one is accepted. (e) CONTEST -- this is the one I would have argued against: twice in two consecutive rounds is a pattern, not an accident; the offline replay proves the block is standalone (it was sourced with a stub `check()`), so a devices gate was cheap this round; and "it would move a coverage pin" is not a cost argument in a round that already moves three census pins and a manifest pin. The one real defense -- keeping both fbctrl probes (PORT and COMMAND, same device, same file) in one place -- argues for splitting BOTH out together, not for leaving the second in the CPU gate. Accept only with a hard stop: `gatedevicesplit` must land before a third device probe enters gate 16.
